<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/intermediate/algorithms-searching-and-sorting">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Computer Science lesson. It builds on the foundational lessons
    <em>Binary and Bits</em> (whose powers of two are what binary search keeps halving
    into) and <em>The Growth of Functions</em> (which introduced Big-O notation as a way
    of comparing growth rates). If those are fresh, nothing else is assumed — every
    algorithm in this lesson is built from scratch on the page.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>turn a problem statement into an algorithm using decomposition, pseudocode and refinement;</li>
    <li>trace linear and binary search, and say when sorting first pays for itself;</li>
    <li>write and amend the five standard patterns: totals, counts, max/min, search and sort;</li>
    <li>compare bubble, insertion, merge and quick sort by growth rate and by best use;</li>
    <li>design normal, boundary and erroneous test data, and use a trace table to hunt down a logic error.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Searching and sorting are the hello-world of algorithms — and the clearest
    demonstration that <em>how</em> you compute matters more than how fast your chip is.
    Same list, same machine: one sorting method grinds through n² comparisons, another
    through n log n. At a million items that is roughly a factor of 50,000 in work; at a
    billion, the difference between days and under a minute. Algorithm choice is a
    hardware decision, and it is free.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lesson also covers the syllabus's craft layer: how a problem becomes an
    algorithm (decomposition, pseudocode, refinement), the standard solution methods
    every course assumes you can write (totals, counts, max/min, search, sort), and the
    testing discipline — normal, boundary and erroneous test data, trace tables, and
    debugging — that turns a working-on-my-examples program into a working program.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="naive vs divide-and-conquer — the curves part company early and never look back" widget="plotter" config={sortCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the two curves track each other closely for small n —
    below about twenty items you can barely separate them. Trace along the x-axis and
    compare the two heights at n = 10, n = 20 and n = 40: the red n² curve pulls away
    steeply while the green n log n curve stays nearly flat, and the gap keeps widening.
    That widening gap, not any single value, is the whole argument of this lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>Designing a solution: problem to algorithm</h2>
  <p>
    The design pipeline has four moves. <strong>Decompose</strong>: split the problem into
    subproblems you can state precisely ("read the scores; find the largest; print it").
    <strong>Specify</strong>: for each piece, write the inputs, the output, and the
    relationship between them — vague steps cannot be coded or tested. <strong>Write
    pseudocode</strong>: the algorithm in structured English (or a flowchart), at a level
    where every step is unambiguous but no language syntax has been chosen — this is
    where the logic lives and where it is cheapest to fix. <strong>Refine</strong>: expand
    any step still too vague until the whole thing could be typed into a program with no
    further thought. The syllabus phrase "writing and amending algorithms" means exactly
    this fluency: producing the pseudocode, then modifying it when the requirement
    changes — which is a skill you should practise literally (take a working algorithm
    and amend it: find the maximum → find the <em>second</em> maximum; search for one
    value → count all matches).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a problem becomes an algorithm through four moves — decompose,
    specify, write pseudocode, refine — and vagueness is cheapest to fix on paper, before
    any language is chosen. Next we stock the toolbox those designs are built from.
  </p>

  <h2>Standard methods: the five patterns</h2>
  <p>
    Nearly every exam-level algorithm is assembled from five standard patterns, and it
    pays to own each as a memorised skeleton with a variable you adapt.
    <strong>Totals</strong>: total ← 0 before the loop; total ← total + value inside.
    <strong>Counting</strong>: same shape, count ← count + 1 when a condition holds.
    <strong>Maximum/minimum</strong>: seed with the first item, then compare each
    newcomer and replace when bigger/smaller — note the seed must be an item of the
    data, not 0 (a max-of-temperatures seeded at 0 fails for winter data, a classic
    boundary bug). <strong>Searching</strong>: walk and compare (linear), or halve
    (binary, below). <strong>Sorting</strong>: bubble, insertion, merge, quick (below).
    Worked example — find the largest of n numbers: read n; read first value into max;
    loop over the remaining n−1 values; if value exceeds max, max ← value; after the
    loop, print max. Every element is there for a reason, and each reason is a place a
    bug could live.
  </p>

  <h2>Linear and binary search</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Linear search</strong> checks every item until it finds the target — simple,
    works on unsorted data, and costs up to n comparisons. <strong>Binary search</strong>
    works only on <em>sorted</em> data and wins by halving: compare the target with the
    middle item; if smaller, discard the upper half; if larger, discard the lower half;
    repeat on what remains. Each comparison halves the candidates, so the count is
    log₂(n): a list of 1,000 needs at most 10; a million needs 20; a billion needs 30.
    That is the phone book's trick, formalised. The prerequisite is the interesting
    engineering trade: sorting costs n log n, so binary searching a list you will query
    once is a loss — but querying it many times is a massive win. Databases, dictionaries,
    and every "sorted by…" column in your life exist because someone planned to search
    repeatedly.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "binary search is always faster, so I should always sort
  first." It wins on comparisons, but sorting costs n log n up front: searching an unsorted
  list once is cheaper with linear search, and sorting only pays when the list will be
  searched many times. The right question is not "which search is faster?" but "how many
  times will I search?".</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> linear search costs up to n comparisons and works on any list;
    binary search costs log₂(n) but only after the data is sorted. That prerequisite is the
    bridge to the next section: how sorting actually gets done.
  </p>

  <h2>Sorting: the four classics</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Bubble sort</strong> repeatedly compares neighbours and swaps when out of
    order, so the largest value "bubbles" to the end each pass — n passes of n
    comparisons, O(n²). <strong>Insertion sort</strong> grows a sorted prefix, sliding
    each new item left to its place — also O(n²) worst case, but excellent on nearly
    sorted data and tiny lists, which is why real libraries use it for the small
    subproblems. <strong>Merge sort</strong> is the divide-and-conquer pattern: split
    the list in half, sort each half recursively, then <em>merge</em> the two sorted
    halves by repeatedly taking the smaller front item. Worked on [4, 2, 7, 1, 3, 8,
    6, 5]: split to [4,2,7,1] and [3,8,6,5]; each splits again to pairs; singles
    merge up — [2,4] from [4],[2]; [1,2,4,7] from [2,4],[1,7]; likewise [3,5,6,8];
    the final merge walks both sorted halves once, and 8 items come out sorted. The
    cost: log₂(8) = 3 levels of splitting, about 8 comparisons of work per level —
    n log n, guaranteed, on every input. <strong>Quick sort</strong> picks a pivot,
    partitions (smaller left, larger right), and recurses — average n log n, worst
    case n² on adversarial pivots, but so cache-friendly in practice that it and its
    descendants (the introsort/hybrids behind Python's sort and Java's
    Arrays.sort are hybrids with insertion sort and heap ideas) run the world's
    default sorts.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> O(n²) means "slow" and O(n log n) means "fast".
  Big-O is growth rate, not milliseconds: for n = 20, an O(n²) algorithm does 400
  operations and is instantly done. The curves only part company at scale — which is
  exactly when it matters, and exactly why you think in growth rates before writing
  code.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the sorting classics trade growth rate against memory and
    stability, and merge sort's n log n is the price of guaranteed speed at scale. But an
    algorithm that is fast yet wrong is worthless — next comes the discipline that finds
    the wrong parts.
  </p>

  <h2>Test data and trace tables</h2>
  <p>
    An algorithm is not done when it runs; it is done when you have attacked it.
    <strong>Normal data</strong> — typical values that exercise the main path.
    <strong>Boundary data</strong> — the edges: empty list, single item, the exact
    limit values, zero, negative numbers; off-by-one errors live here and only here.
    <strong>Erroneous data</strong> — input that should be rejected: a letter where a
    number belongs, a negative age; the program must fail safely, not bizarrely.
    The <strong>trace table</strong> is the paper instrument for logic errors: columns
    for each variable (and the output), one row per loop iteration, values written as
    they change. Tracing the max-finding algorithm on [7, 3, 9, 2] — max: 7, 7, 9, 9 —
    shows exactly when and why each value changed, which is how a wrong answer tells
    you which line is lying. Professionals debug by hand-trace first, debugger second;
    the table forces you to run the code the computer runs, not the code you think
    you wrote.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if the program runs without crashing and passes my
  normal examples, it works." Running is not correctness: the bugs that ship are boundary
  bugs — the empty list, the single item, the value one past the limit — that normal data
  never touches. Boundary and erroneous test data exist precisely because the happy path
  cannot expose them.</p>

  <h2>Correctness is part of the deal</h2>
  <p>
    An algorithm carries an argument that it terminates and produces the right answer —
    and the history of binary search shows how hard the details are. In his 1986
    <em>Programming Pearls</em> column, Jon Bentley asked professional programmers to
    implement binary search and reported that around ninety per cent produced buggy
    versions, usually off-by-one boundary errors. The story has a sequel: in 2006,
    nearly twenty years later, Joshua Bloch found that Java's standard library binary
    search had its own bug — <code>(low + high) / 2</code> overflows when the array
    exceeds about a billion elements, so the fix became the overflow-proof
    <code>low + (high − low) / 2</code>. The lesson generalises: the idea is easy, the
    boundaries are where correctness lives, and even decades-tested library code earns
    its audacity.
  </p>

  <h2>Amending algorithms: the modification drills</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's "writing and amending" is a skill you should drill explicitly, because
    real work is mostly modification. Take the standard skeletons and change them under
    constraint. <strong>Linear search → count all matches</strong>: replace "return on
    first find" with "count ← count + 1 every time the condition holds, never stop early"
    — and notice the early-exit logic was doing double duty. <strong>Max → max and min
    in one pass</strong>: seed two variables from the first item, update each
    independently; the loop structure survives unchanged. <strong>Total → average, safely</strong>:
    divide by the count — but if the count can be zero (empty list), the division is a
    runtime error waiting for boundary test data; guard it. <strong>Binary search →
    first occurrence</strong> (in a list with duplicates): when you find a match, keep
    searching the <em>left</em> half — one character of intent, and a genuinely subtle
    amendment. Each drill teaches the same lesson: understand which part of the
    skeleton carries which responsibility, then move only what must move.
  </p>

  <h2>Efficiency in practice: from growth rates to milliseconds</h2>
  <p>
    The plotter above shows the two curves separating; here is the same fact in counts.
    Sorting 1,000 items: bubble sort, roughly a million comparisons; merge sort, roughly
    10,000. At a million items: 10¹² versus 2×10⁷ — a factor near 50,000. At a billion:
    10¹⁸ versus about 3×10¹⁰ — the difference between decades and half a minute. Two
    practical refinements. First, <strong>measure growth, not seconds</strong>: timing on
    today's machine hides the curve (fast constants flatter bad algorithms at small n);
    doubling n and watching the time quadruple (n²) or barely double (n log n) reveals
    the truth. Second, <strong>constants matter once curves tie</strong>: two O(n log n)
    sorts can differ by 5× in wall-clock time because of memory access patterns — which
    is precisely why quick sort's cache-friendly partitioning beats merge sort in
    practice despite merge sort's better worst case. Algorithm analysis is the map;
    measurement is the terrain; professionals carry both.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading algorithms like a professional</h2>
  <p>
    One more skill completes the lesson: reading other people's algorithms fluently,
    because most of your career you will meet algorithms as code you did not write.
    The professional read has three passes. First, the <strong>contract</strong>: what
    goes in, what comes out, what invariant is claimed ("the list is sorted when the
    loop ends"). Second, the <strong>mechanism</strong>: which standard pattern is this —
    a search, a sort, a traversal with accumulation — and what is the loop actually
    maintaining between iterations? Third, the <strong>edges</strong>: what happens on
    the empty list, the single item, the duplicates, the already-sorted input? Runs of
    real code fail at the third pass far more than the second, which is why the
    boundary-test discipline of this lesson is not exam trivia — it is the professional
    reading habit, taught as an exam syllabus because it is how correctness is actually
    won.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A list of 1,000,000 sorted names is searched with binary search. At most how many comparisons are needed, and why?</li>
    <li>Why must a max-finding algorithm be seeded with the first data item rather than with 0?</li>
    <li>An algorithm passes every example you tried while writing it. Which test-data categories are still missing, and which bugs do they target?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) 20 — each comparison halves the candidates, and a million
    is about 2²⁰, so twenty halvings reach one item. (2) Because the seed must be an item
    of the data: all-negative temperatures would defeat a 0 seed and return a wrong maximum
    — the classic boundary bug. (3) Boundary data (empty list, single item, exact limits)
    and erroneous data (input that should be rejected) — off-by-one errors and unsafe
    failures live there, not on the happy path.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Algorithm choice dominates hardware: n² versus n log n is a factor near 50,000 at a million items.</li>
    <li>Design runs decompose, specify, pseudocode, refine — and vagueness is cheapest to fix on paper.</li>
    <li>Binary search costs log₂(n) but needs sorted data, so sorting first pays only for repeated searches.</li>
    <li>Seed max/min with a real item of the data, never a guess like 0.</li>
    <li>Merge sort's n log n comes from log₂(n) levels of halving with about n merge work per level.</li>
    <li>Attack a finished algorithm with normal, boundary and erroneous test data; use trace tables for logic errors.</li>
    <li>Big-O is a growth rate, not a stopwatch — the curves part company only at scale.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The natural next steps on the intermediate shelf are <em>Programming Fundamentals</em>,
    where these algorithm skeletons become working code, and <em>Data: Files, Tables &amp;
    JSON</em>, which asks where the data being searched and sorted actually lives. Beyond
    them, data structures and algorithm analysis extend this lesson's growth-rate thinking
    to trees, graphs and hash tables.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four expert notes. <strong>The lower bound</strong>: comparison-based sorting cannot
    beat n log n in the worst case — with n! possible orderings, each comparison yields
    one bit, and you need log₂(n!) ≈ n log n bits to decide among them; merge sort is
    optimal, not just good. <strong>Beating it by not comparing</strong>: counting and
    radix sorts exploit known value ranges and sort in O(n) — proof that the n log n
    bound is a property of the method, not of the problem. <strong>Stability</strong>: a
    stable sort preserves the relative order of equal keys — essential when sorting by
    two columns in sequence (sort by name, then stably by department), and a real
    design decision in library sorts (Timsort, Python's default, is stable).
    <strong>Recursion's bill</strong>: divide-and-conquer spends call-stack memory —
    merge sort needs O(n) auxiliary space, quick sort O(log n) average stack — the
    constants that Big-O hides are exactly what systems programmers get paid to
    notice.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming Vol. 3 (Sorting and Searching); Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms; Bentley, Programming Pearls (1986); Bloch, "Extra, Extra — Read All About It: Nearly All Binary Searches and Mergesorts are Broken" (2006). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
